      "transcript": "Hamish Cleeve: Good morning, ClearView consultations. We assess whether an interior window film suits a particular pane and use. To open the booking, could I confirm your surname?\nFfion Devereux: It is Devereux, D-E-V-E-R-E-U-X, rather than the shorter spelling on the web enquiry. Please use Devereux on the appointment.\nHamish Cleeve: Thank you. Which room are we assessing? You can mention another room for future work, but this consultation needs one main window.\nFfion Devereux: The troublesome window is in the home office. The bedroom may need privacy later, but it is not part of this visit.\nHamish Cleeve: And which direction does the office window face? That helps us bring samples suited to the time of day when the strongest light reaches the glass.\nFfion Devereux: It faces west, which is why the strongest sun reaches it later in the day rather than first thing in the morning.\nHamish Cleeve: Could you give me the approximate frame-opening width? The installer will take final pane measurements, so please do not substitute the narrower glass width.\nFfion Devereux: The frame opening is 184 centimetres wide. The glass itself is slightly narrower, but 184 is the figure for the booking.\nHamish Cleeve: I can offer the twenty-third of February, between ten and noon; the twenty-second is already full. Would you like that appointment?\nFfion Devereux: Yes, please book 23 February. I work from home that morning, so the ten-to-noon arrival window is fine.\nHamish Cleeve: I will bring a sample card with several films, allowing you to compare them against the actual daylight before any product is selected or bonded.\nFfion Devereux: Good. A blind makes video calls too dark, so I want to soften the light without losing the view. When should we judge the worst glare?\nHamish Cleeve: Could you tell me when it usually becomes most uncomfortable? Morning brightness can look strong, but the timing in your room is what matters.\nFfion Devereux: The glare is worst in late afternoon, usually after three. It is much less troublesome during the brighter-looking morning.\nHamish Cleeve: Besides reducing glare, what must remain visible from your desk? A fully opaque film gives privacy but may block something you need to see.\nFfion Devereux: The garden must remain visible through the glass. Privacy is useful, but I do not want to lose that view while I am working.\nHamish Cleeve: We can compare neutral and reflective products. Reflective film can look mirrored and its privacy changes after dark. Which type would you prefer to begin with?\nFfion Devereux: I prefer neutral film. I do not want the window to look mirrored, although I understand we should still compare how the samples control glare.\nHamish Cleeve: Before colour or final measurements, I will inspect the glass condition for cracks, seals and any previous coating. That determines whether film is suitable at all.\nFfion Devereux: That is reassuring. I will clear ornaments from the sill but leave the curtains in place so you can see how the room is normally used.\nHamish Cleeve: Exactly. I will email the date and preparation note now. The quotation will follow only after the pane is checked and you have compared the samples in the room. Please leave the curtains in their position because the adviser will compare the pane from both inside and outside. The visit should take about forty minutes, and no glass will be removed during the consultation.",
