      "transcript": "Tavish Beckward: Welcome to Pinewater Reservoir. This briefing covers the marked recreation route, the wildlife boundary and the locations available if conditions change. Check the map before launching; the recording is not a substitute for current ranger advice.\nTavish Beckward: The main launch is on the south shore, beside the visitor centre and equipment check area.\nTavish Beckward: North is at the top. The visitor centre sits on the south shore. The dam occupies the south-west corner and has its own exclusion line; no recreation route enters that area.\nTavish Beckward: The smaller alternative launch is on the west shore. Use it only when the main ramp is closed or a ranger directs your group there.\nTavish Beckward: Conditions can change quickly because open water channels wind along the reservoir. A sheltered inlet may reduce waves, but it does not remove the need for a buoyancy aid or group spacing.\nTavish Beckward: The curved water on the eastern side is the sheltered inlet, which offers protection from a west wind.\nTavish Beckward: The usual circuit travels east from the main ramp, crosses to the portage and continues north. Beginners should turn back at the inlet if the wind warning rises rather than attempting the exposed crossing.\nTavish Beckward: The line of orange buoys in the north-east marks the nesting exclusion zone. Do not pass between those buoys and shore.\nTavish Beckward: Wildlife restrictions protect a seasonal nesting area. The buoys define the boundary on the water. Seeing an empty shore does not permit landing, and paddlers should not approach for photographs.\nTavish Beckward: At the narrow rocky section, leave the water and use the portage path, carrying the canoe to the marked re-entry point.\nTavish Beckward: The portage avoids a narrow shallow reach containing rocks and sensitive bank vegetation. Step out at the signed landing, carry the boat on the firm path and relaunch only at the second sign.\nTavish Beckward: The pale beach below the eastern ridge is the emergency landing beach. It is not a lunch stop.\nTavish Beckward: An emergency landing is marked separately because access for rescue vehicles is possible above the beach. Routine stops there could block that access. Use the radio channel printed on your permit if help is required.\nTavish Beckward: North picnic point is a normal stopping place with racks above the waterline.\nTavish Beckward: Before departure, leave a route card at the visitor centre and report when the group returns. If your route changes, update a ranger whenever communication is available.\nTavish Beckward: The emergency beach is for use only in an emergency such as injury, damaged equipment or rapidly worsening weather.\nTavish Beckward: At the nesting buoy line, no landing is permitted. Stay on the open-water side and keep noise low.\nTavish Beckward: On the portage path, carry boats on foot. Paddling the shallow channel beside it damages the bank.\nTavish Beckward: Group leaders should count paddlers at every stop and maintain visual contact during crossings. If one boat falls behind, the leading canoe waits in sheltered water rather than continuing around a point. Weather flags at the visitor centre use green, amber and red. Amber does not automatically close the route, but it restricts beginner groups to the inlet. Red closes launches. Water temperature can remain low, so spare dry clothing travels in a sealed bag. Each canoe also carries a whistle and a painter line. These items support emergency response but do not make the dam area or wildlife zone safe to enter. At the north point, land only at the signed rack and lift boats clear of waves. The rocky shore immediately east of the point is not an alternative landing. Before entering open water, pairs should agree simple hand signals for stopping and regrouping.\nTavish Beckward: We will now check fitted buoyancy aids and group leaders. Launching begins only after every route card is complete.",
