      "transcript": "Crispin Penrith: Eleri, the supervisor was right: our pilot compared navigation aids only on paper. In practice, participants brought prior knowledge into the task and bypassed the point where the two aids differed.\nEleri Snowe: The pilot failed mainly because participants used a familiar shortcut. They reached the shelf without relying on either information condition.\nCrispin Penrith: The library catalogue assigns a call number, but locating a book also requires interpreting floors, zones, aisles and shelf order. We should study the route without implying that one sign solves every stage.\nEleri Snowe: We cannot exclude every student, but we can use a less familiar floor where the pilot group has not attended classes.\nCrispin Penrith: A between-person design would need a large sample because walking speed varies. A within-person design is more efficient, but order effects become important. Half the participants must begin with the digital prompt and half with the shelf-label condition.\nEleri Snowe: Both conditions must use the same target books. The route display changes, not the item being searched for.\nCrispin Penrith: Target books should be similar in size and placed at ordinary heights. A book displayed face-out or shelved incorrectly would turn the task into a search anomaly rather than a navigation comparison.\nEleri Snowe: Our primary outcome is the time to locate the target book. Questions asked and recall are useful secondary observations.\nCrispin Penrith: Observers should not give hints. If someone asks whether a floor is correct, the observer repeats the task instruction but does not confirm direction. Any spontaneous help from other library users is logged.\nEleri Snowe: A coded route sheet gives observers the same categories, so they record corridor and aisle choices consistently.\nCrispin Penrith: Completion time begins when the call number is handed over and ends when the participant touches the correct book. Pauses to ask questions remain part of the time because they reflect the navigation process.\nEleri Snowe: At the start of each task, the participant receives one printed call number rather than a title and cover image.\nCrispin Penrith: The coded path sheet divides the route into choice points. That lets us see whether an aid prevents a wrong floor, a wrong zone or only a wrong aisle. A single total time would hide those patterns.\nEleri Snowe: In the digital condition, the phone displays a location prompt with floor, zone and shelf direction.\nCrispin Penrith: Accessibility should be checked before recruitment. The route must not require stairs, and the phone text must support enlargement. Those are delivery requirements, not extra experimental conditions.\nEleri Snowe: Observers mark every wrong aisle entered, even if the participant leaves it quickly.\nCrispin Penrith: Finally, the debrief explains that the study evaluates information design, not individual intelligence. We delete the link between names and route data after scheduling is complete.\nEleri Snowe: After each task, a short survey asks about perceived difficulty, which may differ from measured completion time.\nEleri Snowe: I will handle the route sheet, while Crispin prepares the random order in which conditions and target books appear.\nCrispin Penrith: We should also capture whether participants consult the catalogue again during the task. Rechecking may indicate uncertainty at the transition from floor information to shelf order. The digital condition must not reveal a photograph of the target shelf, because that would add a visual recognition cue absent from the printed condition. Battery level and network access are practical risks. The prompt should be stored locally on the study phone so a connection failure does not create unequal delays. Participants use the same device and text size unless an accessibility adjustment is requested and recorded. Observers require training with two example routes before collecting data. Agreement can be checked by having both code the same practice video and discussing differences in aisle and hesitation categories. If an observer misses a movement, the record should say missing rather than guessing from the final location. That approach may reduce the dataset slightly but protects the meaning of each coded choice. A short rehearsal should also confirm that the timer, route code and observer notes all refer to the same trial. That check prevents a correctly recorded walk from being attached to the wrong condition during later analysis.\nEleri Snowe: Once Crispin randomises the order, we can run two internal trials and confirm that neither condition contains an accidental clue.",
