      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct letter for each item.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Animal navigation uses several cues, and today’s topic is one that humans do not normally notice without instruments: the polarisation pattern of skylight. It supplies directional structure even when the sun is difficult to see.\nLecturer: Direct sunlight becomes scattered by molecules in the atmosphere, creating directional information across the dome of the sky.\nLecturer: Polarisation describes the orientation of light-wave oscillation. Scattering in the sky produces a geometrical pattern related to the sun’s position. The pattern is not equally strong in every direction or weather condition.\nLecturer: That scattering produces a pattern of sky polarisation: light waves tend to oscillate in particular orientations depending on position.\nLecturer: Many navigating insects have a specialised dorsal region of the compound eye. Receptors there differ in orientation, allowing the nervous system to compare responses and extract the direction of polarised light.\nLecturer: Insects detect these orientations with specialised photoreceptors, often concentrated in a particular region of the compound eye.\nLecturer: Detection is only the first step. A compass must connect the sky signal with a desired travel direction. An insect leaving a nest and one returning to it may use the same cue but choose opposite bearings.\nLecturer: Signals from those receptors are compared in neural circuits to produce a celestial compass rather than a picture of a painted line.\nLecturer: Time compensation matters because the sun and associated sky pattern appear to move. Without an internal clock, a fixed visual rule would gradually point in the wrong geographical direction during a long journey.\nLecturer: The pattern may remain useful under partial cloud cover, when the solar disc itself is not visible but portions of sky can still be sampled.\nLecturer: Experiments manipulate one cue while attempting to preserve others. A filter can rotate the polarisation pattern above an arena. Screens can hide the sun. Eye covers can remove input from selected receptors.\nLecturer: Because the sky pattern changes through the day, the insect compares direction with an internal clock to maintain a useful bearing.\nLecturer: Each manipulation has limits. A filter may alter intensity as well as orientation. A screen may leave landscape cues. An eye cover could affect general vision. Control groups are therefore essential.\nLecturer: In a rotating-filter experiment, changing the artificial polarisation axis produces a corresponding change in chosen direction.\nLecturer: Cloud is not a simple on-off switch. Thin or broken cloud may leave usable patches, while uniform dense cloud reduces the signal. Some insects then weight wind, landmarks or magnetic information more heavily.\nLecturer: A screened-sun experiment tests whether an insect can orient when the sun itself is hidden but a patch of polarised sky remains available.\nLecturer: Species also differ. A mechanism demonstrated in one insect should not automatically be assigned to all flying or walking insects. Ecology, eye structure and journey length shape which cue is useful.\nLecturer: A clock-shift experiment alters the animal’s internal time, helping separate compensation by the clock from a fixed response to the visual cue.\nLecturer: The strongest conclusion comes from converging evidence: receptor anatomy, neural responses and predictable behavioural changes under controlled cue rotations. No single observation establishes the whole navigation system.\nLecturer: Covering the specialised eye region, while leaving other vision available, can confirm a role for those receptors if orientation deteriorates.\nLecturer: The geometry of the polarisation pattern also creates ambiguity. A compass reading may have a one-hundred-and-eighty-degree alternative unless another cue distinguishes the two directions. The animal’s travel goal, brightness gradient or landmark information can help resolve that ambiguity. Behavioural arenas simplify the sky but can introduce reflections from walls. Experiments therefore use matte surfaces and control the intensity of light while rotating only the intended axis. Outdoors, researchers may release insects from unfamiliar sites and record the initial orientation before landmarks become familiar. An initial bearing is informative, but successful navigation over a long distance involves further decisions that the first measurement does not capture. Neural recordings provide a different scale of evidence. Some cells change activity with the e-vector orientation of light, while others represent heading after several sensory inputs have been combined. Linking those signals to free behaviour remains challenging. Polarised light is best understood as one weighted input in a flexible system. When it is reliable, it can stabilise direction. When cloud or vegetation weakens it, an insect need not become completely lost if wind, odour or learnt landmarks are available. This cue integration explains why removing one source of information sometimes changes orientation strongly and sometimes has only a modest effect. Researchers should also report the weather and horizon visibility so that field results can be compared across sites.\nLecturer: Next we will examine how insects combine this celestial compass with landmarks when they approach a familiar nest site.",
