      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A. A long epic performed without a written script can look like an impossible feat of memory. Early observers often assumed that the singer had memorised a fixed text word for word. Yet comparison between performances revealed both recurrence and variation. This suggested a different model: the performer could compose during delivery by drawing on familiar phrases, scenes and narrative patterns. Memory remained crucial, but it stored resources and relationships rather than a single unchanging sequence.\n\nB. A formula is a recurring expression suited to a particular metrical position and idea. It gives the singer language that already fits the rhythm, reducing the number of decisions required at speed. Formulaic diction is not necessarily a sign of laziness or poor originality. It is part of the technique that makes fluent composition possible. A skilled performer can select, adapt and combine inherited material while responding to audience, occasion and the direction of the story.\n\nC. Recurring themes operate at a broader scale. A departure, challenge, feast or recognition scene may have a familiar sequence of actions without identical wording. These frameworks help the performer maintain narrative coherence while varying detail and emphasis. The same theme can be brief in one performance and elaborated in another. This flexibility explains how an epic can remain recognisable across occasions without existing as one fixed verbal object.\n\nD. When a performance is transcribed, a moving event becomes a written snapshot. The page preserves valuable evidence, but readers may treat accidental features of that occasion as definitive. Pauses, audience reactions and musical delivery can also be difficult to represent. A transcript is therefore both record and transformation. Comparing several recordings or texts can reveal which elements are stable and which belong to the performer’s immediate choices.\n\nE. In a literate tradition, deviation from a printed poem may be called an error. In oral composition, variation can demonstrate competence if it preserves the story’s logic and style. Freedom is not unlimited: metre, audience expectation and inherited narrative impose creative constraint. Expertise consists in negotiating those limits. The performer must be sufficiently traditional to remain intelligible and sufficiently responsive to make the event work in the present.\n\nF. Such skill is often acquired through prolonged listening and participation rather than through memorising a manual. An apprentice absorbs recurrent patterns, attempts shorter performances and gradually develops control over longer sequences. The process resembles learning a generative language. Knowing many expressions matters, but so does knowing when they fit. This account helps explain why direct translation of a formula may miss its practical role inside a metrical and cultural system.\n\nG. Formulaic composition complicates modern ideas of authorship. A performance is produced by an individual, yet its resources have been shaped by generations. Calling it either wholly original or merely repeated is inadequate. The singer’s contribution lies in selection, arrangement, pacing and adaptation within a distributed tradition. Scholars must therefore attend to both continuity and agency. The theory does not deny artistic individuality; it locates individuality in a different relation to inherited language.\n\nH. Not every repeated phrase proves oral composition, and not every oral tradition works in the same way. Formula counts can be informative, but they need contextual evidence about metre, training and performance. A model developed from one body of epic cannot simply be imposed on all narrative cultures. The enduring contribution of the theory is more modest and more useful: it shows how recurrence can support live creation rather than merely indicate mechanical recall.\n\nI. Performance is also an exchange with listeners. Their familiarity can permit a singer to compress a well-known episode, while a ceremonial occasion may encourage elaboration. Responses during delivery can influence pacing and emphasis. These effects are difficult to recover from a silent transcript, which is why recordings and contextual notes matter. The audience does not necessarily dictate the story, but it forms part of the environment in which choices are made. Recognising this helps explain why variation is not random noise around an ideal text. Some variation is a purposeful adjustment to the social work that the epic is doing on a particular occasion. The setting can affect memory as well. Music, repeated openings and the expectation of a sequence provide cues that are absent when lines are quoted in isolation. Researchers who remove every performance feature may therefore simplify the very mechanism they hope to study. A full account connects verbal formula, narrative theme, rhythm, interaction and occasion rather than treating the words as a detached database.",
